### Current Behavior

Connectivity via peer relays only work when nodes can themselves access/see the peer relay.

### Expected Behavior

Peer relays should work even if nodes themselves can't access/see the peer relay node. SaaS does not require such an [additional rule to gain visibility](https://tailscale.com/docs/features/peer-relay#step-2-create-a-peer-relay-policy).

<img width="1403" height="561" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a6de73e6-5fb8-428d-a3bc-901165dc801c" />

### Steps To Reproduce

Given this policy:

```json
{
  "hosts": {
    "n1": "100.64.0.1/32",
    "n2": "100.64.0.2/32",
    "peer-relay": "100.64.0.3/32"
  },
  "grants": [
    {
      "src": ["n1"],
      "dst": ["n2"],
      "ip": ["*"]
    },
    {
      "src": ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst": ["peer-relay"],
      "app": {
        "tailscale.com/cap/relay": []
      }
    }
  ]
}
``` 

1. Create a new headscale user
1. Add three nodes for the user, `n1` and `n2`, `peer-relay`
1. Ping `n2` from `n1`: `tailscale ping -c 0 100.64.0.2`
1. Make sure that `n1` can't directly reach `n2` (firewalling, debug options, etc). This can be verified via `tailscale ping` and `DERP(region)` in the output.
1. Enable peer relay on `peer-relay` node: `tailscale set --relay-server-port=40000`
1. Observe that the peer-relay is not selected, connection stays with DERP.

The above setup works on SaaS.

However, it starts to work when `n1`, `n2` are aware of the `peer-relay` node using a icmp dummy grant. The modified policy in full:

```json5
{
  "hosts": {
    "n1": "100.64.0.1/32",
    "n2": "100.64.0.2/32",
    "peer-relay": "100.64.0.3/32"
  },
  "grants": [
    {
      // workaround required to get peer relay connectivity to work
      "src": ["n1", "n2"],
      "dst": ["peer-relay"],
      "ip": ["icmp:*"]
    },
    {
      "src": ["n1"],
      "dst": ["n2"],
      "ip": ["*"]
    },
    {
      "src": ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst": ["peer-relay"],
      "app": {
        "tailscale.com/cap/relay": []
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

With this policy, the connection immediately upgrades from DERP to a peer-relayed connection.

### Environment

